<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Takeda clan domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1519 CE – 1582 CE (approx.) · capital Kofu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The mountain domain of the Takeda of Kai, whose lord Takeda Shingen was one of the most feared commanders of Japan's Sengoku era.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-nobutora">Takeda Nobutora</a> unified the province of Kai after years of fighting local rivals and in 1519 founded a new seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsutsujigasaki-castle">Tsutsujigasaki</a>, around which the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kofu">Kofu</a> grew. In 1541 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Harunobu</a>, later known as Shingen, exiled him and took over.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Shingen</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinano-province">Shinano</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suruga-province">Suruga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kozuke-province">Kozuke</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totomi-province">Totomi</a>, fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a> five times at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-kawanakajima">Kawanakajima</a>. He built flood-control dykes, the Shingen-zutsumi, developed gold mines and issued a house code. His cavalry and organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> bands made Kai a military power, and he crushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mikatagahara">Mikatagahara</a> in 1573.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Shingen</a> died in 1573. His son Katsuyori lost much of his army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-clan">Oda</a> and Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nagashino">Nagashino</a> in 1575; in 1582 their forces invaded, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> defected, and Katsuyori killed himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tenmokuzan">Tenmokuzan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/takeda-clan-domain.jpg" alt="Takeda clan domain" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Mukai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Crest_Takeda_Hisi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1519 CE – 1582 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kofu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Takeda Nobutora, Takeda Shingen, Takeda Katsuyori</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Takeda clan, Takeda of Kai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1550&amp;focus=takeda-clan-domain" aria-label="Takeda clan domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8180 889 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Takeda clan domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8500 1145L8448 1163"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8448 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8500 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8500 1145h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kofu. <a href="/atlas?year=1550&amp;focus=takeda-clan-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
